{# The SCOPED twin of op-cr-flat/op-cr-nested. `must_match_fields` exists twice in the
   protos — once on the classic ProvisionTokenV2 (api/proto/.../types.proto) and once on
   the scoped ScopedToken (api/proto/teleport/scopes/joining/v1/token.proto) — and crdgen
   generates a SEPARATE CRD for each. Both were broken identically, and both are fixed by
   the same schemagen.go change, but nothing here would notice if only one of the two
   generated CRDs were regenerated: teleportprovisiontokens and teleportscopedtokensv1
   are independent files, and `make crd-manifests-diff` only compares what the current
   generator produces against what is checked in.

   No join test: scoped generic_oidc joining is already covered end-to-end by the
   `generic_oidc` module.

   RECONCILE IS GATED, NOT COMPLETED, and the checks say so. The reconciler does run — an
   unscoped operator starts every reconciler whose CRD is installed
   (controllers/resources/setup.go filterEnabledReconcilers) — but since RFD 328 (#69074,
   in master and v18 alike) reconcilers/generic.go checkScope sets ValidScope=False
   ("Resource is scoped but operator is not. Refusing to reconcile.") and returns BEFORE
   the upsert, so this CR never reaches Teleport. What is asserted is therefore CR-level:
   the generated scopedtokensv1 CRD accepts the nested Struct, and the operator decodes it
   (ValidStructure=True) on the way to the gate. That still exercises the crdgen change,
   which is what this fixture is for.

   Restoring the full round trip needs a SECOND, scoped operator deployment — it cannot be
   this one, because a scoped operator refuses UNSCOPED resources (the mirror case in
   checkScope), i.e. every other CR in this module. Such a deployment needs:
     * `-scope /opscope -owner-email <addr>`, plus KUBE_NAMESPACE and RELEASE_NAME in the
       env (the helm chart injects both; scoped mode reads them via operator/state, and
       exits at startup without them).
     * a SCOPED join token. The harness bot token will not do: tbot rejects the identity
       with "received unscoped identity upon join, but tbot is configured in scoped mode"
       (lib/tbot/internal/identity/service.go checkScopeCorrectness). The operator chart's
       values.yaml states the requirement outright. Note each attempt CONSUMES the
       single-use bot token, so a crashlooping scoped operator cannot retry.

   Requires TELEPORT_UNSTABLE_SCOPES=yes on auth (set via auth_env in render.yaml). The
   operator bot's `editor` role already grants scoped_token RW (lib/services/presets.go),
   so authorization is not what blocks the write here. #}
apiVersion: resources.teleport.dev/v1
kind: TeleportScopedTokenV1
metadata:
  name: op-scoped-token
  namespace: teleport
scope: "{{ scoped_token_scope }}"
spec:
  roles: [Node]
  assigned_scope: "{{ scoped_token_scope }}/agents"
  join_method: generic_oidc
  usage_mode: unlimited
  generic_oidc:
    issuer: "https://oidc.{{ lab_domain }}:8443"
    audience: "{{ fqdn }}/generic-oidc"
    must_match_fields:
      # flat: worked even with the old `additionalProperties: true` schema
      org: ethernet-fyi
      # nested: pruned by the old schema, preserved by the fixed one
      nested:
        deep: {{ scoped_nested_value }}
    allow_any:
      - conditions:
          - attribute: sub
            eq: {value: "{{ oidc_subject }}"}
